---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: site.name,
  email: `mailto:${site.email}`,
  jobTitle: site.role,
  address: { '@type': 'PostalAddress', addressLocality: site.location, addressCountry: 'VN' },
};
---

<Base
  title={`About — ${site.name}`}
  description="Self-taught by being quizzed: how I learn, what I work on in Ho Chi Minh City, and the kind of argument I find interesting."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="shell page">
    <header class="page__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>I learned nearly all of it by being quizzed.</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        Ho Chi Minh City. Web development, security and LLM pipelines on one
        side; English-teaching material for Vietnamese students on the other.
        Those halves are not two hobbies that happen to share a person — the
        test-extraction work on the home page exists precisely where they meet.
      </p>

      <h2>No credential, an actual curriculum</h2>
      <p>
        I am not certified in any of this. What I have instead is a method, and
        it is narrower than "self-taught" usually means: I learn things by being
        quizzed on them and then grading myself honestly.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that looks like TryHackMe rooms worked through topic by
        topic, redone from the start after a bad score rather than moved past.
        It looks like writing my own grammar exercises, finishing them in a
        scratchpad, and only then checking against the answer key I wrote. It
        looks like learning TypeScript by mapping every concept onto Python I
        already knew, so the new thing had somewhere to attach.
      </p>
      <p>
        The failure mode this avoids is the one where you read a tutorial,
        follow along, feel competent, and cannot reproduce any of it a week
        later. Grading yourself honestly is the whole trick, and it is
        unpleasant often enough that most people skip it.
      </p>

      <h2>How I argue</h2>
      <p>
        I ask for sources at the level a paper would need, and I check effect
        sizes against the claims made about them. An anomaly with an R² of 0.6%
        is not a strong relationship no matter how small the p-value is; a
        sample of 28 does not support "there is nothing here". Both of those
        sentences are on this site because I had to write them about my own
        results.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not take framings at face value. A loud ego is not the only kind —
        ego is about gravity rather than volume, about how much the self bends
        what gets perceived. Criticism is usually a more credible form of
        loyalty than praise is. I have argued both of those at length and I will
        again.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am also skeptical of my own field's hype cycles. I spent a while
        questioning whether vector databases and retrieval pipelines still earn
        their complexity now that context windows are enormous, and landed on
        "give the model a table of contents and let it ask" before I found out
        that approach had a name.
      </p>
      <p>
        One specific thing: I do not accept US regulatory or market framing as
        the default. Vietnam has its own disclosure deadlines, its own
        transaction taxes, its own absence of retail stock borrowing. When a
        study is built on US data I want that said, not implied to transfer.
      </p>

      <h2>The disproportionate interest</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-domain pattern transfer, and specifically what playing drums does
        to a brain and whether any of it carries onto a badminton court.
        Corpus callosum thickness in drummers, four-limb independence, timing
        circuitry, whether motor learning of that kind shows up as reading an
        opponent's patterns earlier.
      </p>
      <p>
        I will read the actual neuroscience and then argue about whether the
        sample sizes justify the claims, which is usually where that
        conversation ends up and is the part I enjoy.
      </p>

      <h2>What I am doing now</h2>
      <p>
        Quantitative research on Vietnamese equities — a factor and strategy
        pipeline with a weekly pass scheduled but not yet run end to end,
        currently one strategy screened to a negative result and one separate
        study with a positive one. Test
        material and platform work on the teaching side. Both are written up on
        the <a href="/">home page</a>, including the parts that did not work.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want to talk about any of it, <a href="/contact/">contact</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: var(--s-5) var(--s-7); }
  .page__head {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    padding-bottom: var(--s-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .page__head h1 { font-size: var(--step-3); letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
</style>
